Ok.

> Is it a case where if one has a BIOS (on-card in this case) that can do
> LBA48 then  that'll allow the driver to work with the BIOS instructions
> directly, bypassing the lack-of-LBA48-in-the-driver issue? TIA for
> helping me to better understand this issue.

Alright, so here is the thing: nowadays almost nothing uses the bios to
"talk" to the card. It is way too slow. The bios is only there to get the
card recognized by the computer during the boot process so you can boot
from it (ie ide or scsi cards) or get some output (video card). The end
result of that is even if the bios on the card knows about LBA48 it doesn't
mean the driver does.

> No. So... are you implying that if I compile support for it in the kernel
> that the OS will detect it at boot-time? That would be great. I did
> compile in support for the PDC20268 (althouh the TX2 is a PDC20269). Maybe
> it'll work. Please advise if I should try it again. It didn't work last
> time, but then again I hadn't compiled in the non-default PDC20268 driver.

Well the detection by SRM is only useful if you want SRM to be able to boot
from the devices connected to this card but it doesn't mean the OS (Linux
in our case) won't be able to us it. Known examples are video cards that
are unrecognized by SRM but work fine with XFree.

So if I get you correctly (and the quote from Jason Garrell) I assume the
driver knows about LBA48 so yes my understanding is that it should work.
So give it a shot and let us know.
