ijjftx@hotmail.com (Ping) writes:

> The display problem came back again today. It happened when a screen
> saver was running after the machine was idle for a while. When I
> touched the mouse to resume working with the machine, the screen saver
> turned into broken display on screen, with many tiny images of the
> xterms that were opened in desktop. I had to switch to and from linux
> console to recover. This time I was running the latest XFree86 4.3.0.

Did it recover without rebooting or killing any processes?  I've seen
the screen image get messed up when I was doing some low-level program
of the graphics chip that interfered with X.  Also running fbset when
X is active can cause strange things.  Are you using the framebuffer
console?

> One thing related to the problem that puzzles me is the color depth
> setting in the file XF86Config-4. I have been using 24. The release
> page of XFree86 4.3.0 recommends 32 for the driver glint, claiming
> that 24 has problem. But when I switched to 32, the X server didn't
> start at all, instead it gave an error message complaining that the
> depth 32 is not supported by the driver! So I have to stick with 24,
> which apparantly has problems known to me and to the people who
> prepared the XFree86 release note, although I don't know whether the
> problems are same or not. Anyone has any clue? Thanks.

You should have 24 in your configuration file.  The driver will use 32
bits per pixel with 24 color bits and 8 scrap bits.  You should see
something like "(--) Depth 24 pixmap format is 32 bpp" in your X
server log file.  Packed 24 bit pixels don't work.


