Mathieu Chouquet-Stringer <mathieu@newview.com> wrote:
> .... if I get his message correctly, the
> board is or was designed with some kind of alphabios (and not srm) in mind.

Alphabios (ARC and other derivatives really) is another set of firmware
for Alpha boards which was created to load NT.  The original was SRM
which was written for needs of VMS and later adapted to support Unix
as well.  Both firmware families have really a number of subtypes
and versions which differ in their capabilities.  Linux can boot from
either (you need different loaders) but SRM is usually preferable for
various reasons.

Some boards have only one type of firmware available and some both.  LX
belongs to the later category although NVRAM is too small to make it
possible to have two consoles in it at the same time (there are boards
where this is possible) so if you are switching then you have to reload
firmware from an external media.  On every LX I tried that this was not
a problem.  If a modified proprietary loader of your board is able to
put into NVRAM any of these I have no idea but if you will manage to
load one then the other should be a reload away.

  Michal


